\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Examples of shoreline misidentification in a macrotidal beach (UTM ETRS89 30N) (adapted from \citealp {cabezas_site-specific_2026})}}{13}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Gantt chart of the project timeline.}}{17}{}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of a beach profile illustrating shoreline indicators.(1) Feature based, detected on beach face: HTSL—high-tide swash line; WDL—wet–dry line; HWL—high-water line; IWL—instantaneous water line. (2) Elevation-based: MHW—mean high-water, MSL—mean sea level; MLW—mean low-water. (Source: \citealp {valverde_historical_2025})}}{20}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces (a) Timeline for the publicly available satellites with global coverage. Source: Own elaboration, adapted and updated from \citet {vos_sub-annual_2019}. (b) Rapid evolution of the number of publications on satellite-derived shorelines (Number of documents per year for documents that include keywords ``satellite'' AND ``shoreline'' in Scopus database).}}{23}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Schematic diagram of spectral index calculations using Sentinel-2 bands.}}{24}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces Cross-section of a macrotidal beach showing the spectral signature of water, white-water, wet sand, sand and land, derived from a Sentinel-2 image. The dashed line represents the shoreline extracted using MNDWI and Otsu's thresholding method, whereas the solid line represents the actual waterline. The MNDWI index is not able to differentiate between wet sand and water, leading to a landward bias in the extracted shoreline up to 120 m.}}{26}{}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces General methodological workflow of the study. The diagram outlines the data acquisition, model training, extraction pipelines, and evaluation stages detailed throughout this chapter.}}{30}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Location of the study sites: Salinas (left) and El Puntal (right) beaches. RGB image (top), topobathymetry (middle) using the ETRS89 / UTM 30N coordinate system, and beach profile (bottom) highlighting the intertidal range defined by the HAT/LAT (Highest/Lowest Astronomical Tide). (Source: \citealp {cabezas_site-specific_2026})}}{32}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Comparison between the continuous oceanographic time-series (purple) and the conditions captured exactly during Sentinel-2 overpasses (yellow dots) for Salinas beach. The top panels highlight the Astronomical Tide, demonstrating the observational bias caused by the sun-synchronous orbit that systematically trims the high-tide peaks. The bottom panels show the Significant Wave Height ($H_s$), which more closely follows the theoretical distribution, albeit missing the extreme storm events.}}{34}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Screenshot of the DigitalSreeni Image Annotator software interface (Salinas beach). The overlaid mask displays the four annotated classes: water (yellow), white-water (red), wet sand (purple), and sand (cyan).}}{35}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Screenshot of the ArcGIS Pro interface (El Puntal beach). The manually digitized waterline (black dashed line) is overlaid on the Sentinel-2 RGB composite.}}{36}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Diagram illustrating the waterline extraction pipeline for traditional spectral indices. The process involves a three-step sequence: (1) calculation of the continuous spectral index raster, (2) application of the cutoff threshold to create a binary mask, and (3) extraction of the waterline vector using the Marching Squares contouring algorithm.}}{41}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.7}{\ignorespaces Example of the pixel-based evaluation mask. The left panel shows the original RGB image. The dynamic evaluation buffer (right) highlights the 10-pixel zone around the GT waterline where the pixel-based metrics are calculated, effectively excluding the deep water and inland areas from the evaluation.}}{44}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces Example of the perpendicular transect methodology for geometric evaluation. The black line represents the manually digitized GT waterline, while the purple line represents the predicted waterline from either the DL or index-based method (in this case, MNDWI using Zero threshold). The yellow line is the fixed landward baseline. The cyan lines are the perpendicular transects cast from the baseline to the predicted and GT waterlines. The offset is calculated as the absolute distance between the intersection points of the predicted and GT waterlines along each transect.}}{46}{}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Comparison of the sand reflectance metrics between El Puntal and Salinas. The left panel displays the absolute albedo proxy ($\bar {\rho }_{RGB}$), while the right panel highlights the colorimetric slope ($\Delta _{R-B}$). Boxplots represent the interquartile range and median, with overlaid stripplots detailing individual scene observations.}}{50}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Pixel-based performance distributions (IoU and Dice coefficient) for the four annotated coastal classes, disaggregated by study area. The networks demonstrate exceptional accuracy for standard macro-features (Water, Sand) and maintain highly robust predictions for the morphodynamically complex Wet Sand. The performance drop in White Water at El Puntal is directly attributed to the physical scarcity of wave foam in the sheltered estuarine environment.}}{51}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Binary classification performance (Water vs. Non-water) and architectural efficiency. The right panel displays the Mean IoU on a standard 0-1 scale, highlighting the generalized predictive saturation across all models. The left panel maps the Mean IoU against the total parameter count, demonstrating that highly efficient encoders ($\sim $25M parameters, such as MiT-B2) systematically outperform their heavier, overparameterized counterparts ($>$60M parameters).}}{53}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Distribution of cross-shore extraction offsets by model family. The left panel (Boxplot) displays the aggregated global performance, highlighting the median and interquartile range (IQR). The right panel (Violin plot) disaggregates the errors by study area (El Puntal vs. Salinas), emphasizing the severe performance degradation of index-based methods in complex environments compared to the geographic robustness of DL models.}}{54}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Mean Absolute Error (MAE) heatmaps for intra-family configuration assessment. The left panel details the performance of spectral indices across different thresholding techniques, highlighting extreme variance. The right panel displays the Deep Learning configurations, demonstrating high stability and the superiority of the U-Net (MiT-B2) hybrid architecture.}}{56}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Hexbin error distributions for Index-based models. The plots map the predicted shoreline position (SDW) against the reference waterline (RWL). The dashed white line represents the 1:1 perfect agreement. Most indices show a severe Landward bias (wet sand confusion), while NDWI shows a Seaward bias (foam confusion). SCoWI (LM Otsu) stands out as the most accurate traditional method.}}{58}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Hexbin error distributions for Deep Learning semantic segmentation models. In contrast to the traditional baseline, all DL configurations demonstrate minimal dispersion, uniformly tight clustering around the 1:1 bisector ($R^2 \ge 0.96$), and the complete elimination of extreme cross-shore biases.}}{59}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Alongshore variability of extraction offsets for Index-based models. The upper map panels reference the geographical location of the cross-shore transects. The boxplots show the error distribution per \texttt {transect\_id} compared to the overall site performance (right sub-panels).}}{61}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces Alongshore variability of extraction offsets for Deep Learning models. The upper map panels reference the geographical location of the cross-shore transects. The boxplots show the error distribution per \texttt {transect\_id} compared to the overall site performance (right sub-panels).}}{62}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces Relative performance sensitivity to hydrodynamic forcing. The plots display the percentage change in the median offset ($\Delta \%$) relative to the overall site median across astronomical tide (left) and significant wave height (right) quartiles. Absolute median values are annotated for each quartile to contextualize the operational impact of these relative changes.}}{64}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces Cross-site generalization evaluated through the difference in Mean Absolute Error ($\Delta $ MAE) between El Puntal and Salinas. Values closer to the zero vertical line indicate higher robustness to changing environmental conditions. The DL-based models (right panel) demonstrate near-perfect generalization compared to the highly site-dependent Index-based baseline (left panel).}}{65}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.12}{\ignorespaces Overall multi-criteria performance heatmap. The matrix displays the absolute values for MAD, IQR, MAE, Bias, RMSE, and $R^2$ across all evaluated models. The best absolute value for each metric is highlighted in bold, showcasing the Deep Learning dominance and the anomalous central-tendency precision of the SCoWI (LM Otsu) index.}}{67}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.13}{\ignorespaces Comprehensive bump chart ranking based on the six geometric and statistical metrics (1 = Best Performance). The arithmetic mean of these ranks dictates the global hierarchy, highlighting the Top 3 global architectures (U-Net MiT-B2, DeepLabV3+ MiT-B4, and U-Net EfficientNet-B4).}}{68}{}%
